# Physics REU 2026 — Biaggio Research Group, Lehigh University

Undergraduate research conducted Summer 2026 in the Biaggio Research Group (Lehigh University) 
as part of the NSF REU program. This repository documents experimental work, data analysis 
scripts, and results across three projects investigating singlet fission, triplet-pair spin dynamics, 
and nonlinear optics in organic semiconductors.

## Research Context

Singlet fission is a process in which a photoexcited singlet exciton (S₁) splits into two 
spin-entangled triplet excitons (T₁+T₁). Because the triplet pair begins in a net spin-0 state, 
its spin wavefunction evolves coherently under the triplet-pair Hamiltonian — the sum of the 
zero-field splitting (ZFS) term and the Zeeman term from any applied magnetic field. When the 
pair re-encounters, fusion back to a singlet (and thus photon emission) requires spin-0 character, 
so the time-oscillating singlet probability directly modulates fluorescence — producing measurable 
quantum beats in time-resolved photoluminescence (TDPL) data.

This work builds on a recently published result from the group (Rex, Pham Xuan, Curran, Biaggio, 
*On the amplitude of fluorescence quantum beats from the fusion of entangled triplet exciton 
pairs*) and focuses on extending that framework to magnetic-field-dependent PL enhancement, 
cryogenic transport measurements, and a proof-of-concept ultrafast detection scheme.

## Projects

### Project 1 — Magnetic Field Induced Photoluminescence Enhancement
Measures how an applied magnetic field changes total photon emission and the time-resolved 
fluorescence shape in rubrene, tetracene, and DPH. The field shifts spin eigenstate energies, 
altering the integrated singlet-regeneration probability and thus the fluorescence yield. Key 
variables: field strength, field orientation (a/b/c axes and ab-plane rotation), temperature, 
excitation density (geminate vs. non-geminate triplet-pair populations), and pulsed vs. CW 
illumination.

PL enhancement is quantified as ΔPL/PL₀ = [PL(B) − PL(0)] / PL(0).

TDPL traces are fit with a bi-exponential + bi-power-law baseline; quantum beats are isolated 
by dividing out the fit and analyzed as a decaying sinusoid to extract the TID dephasing rate 
k_TID and beat frequency.

### Project 2 — Low Temperature TDPL with Cryostat
Repeats the TDPL protocol at cryogenic temperatures (down to 4 K via liquid helium cryostat) 
for rubrene and tetracene. Lower temperature reduces phonon scattering, extending spin 
coherence times and potentially revealing beats that are washed out at room temperature. Also 
probes how triplet diffusion rates — and therefore Transport-Induced Dephasing (TID) severity — 
change with temperature, testing whether TID weakens when hopping slows.

### Project 3 — Sum Frequency Generation (SFG) *(exploratory)*
Proof-of-concept for an ultrafast TDPL detection scheme. A 1030 nm probe pulse combines with 
rubrene's ~600 nm emission in a nonlinear crystal to produce a UV SFG signal (~374 nm). 
Because SFG intensity depends on the instantaneous fluorescence intensity at the probe's 
arrival time, scanning the probe path length maps out the fluorescence decay with picosecond 
resolution — far beyond the timing jitter of standard TCSPC electronics. First milestone: confirm 
detection of a UV SFG signal.

## Techniques
- Time-Correlated Single Photon Counting (TCSPC / TDPL)
- Physical Vapor Transport (PVT) crystal growth — rubrene, tetracene, DPH
- Cryostat operation (liquid helium, 4 K)
- Sum Frequency Generation (SFG)
- Optical microscopy for crystal characterization

## Analysis
Scripts in Python and MATLAB for:
- Bi-exponential + bi-power-law TDPL fitting
- Quantum beat extraction and decaying sinusoid fitting
- k_TID vs. (f_AA − f_BB)² analysis to extract 2D triplet diffusion time τ₂D
- PL enhancement ratio calculation across field/temperature/density sweeps

